number|title|condition|evidence|remediation|limitation|attack|nist|nist_rationale|sources|severity
001|A source-account control path lacks required directory-change auditing|A confirmed privileged source-account modification path exists AND required effective audit policy or object SACL coverage for its relevant changes is absent.|Underlying account path; applicable event categories; effective audit policy; object SACL; collection coverage; logging requirement.|Enable the required directory-change auditing and protect its destination.|No recent events is not evidence auditing is disabled. Missing permission to inspect SACLs produces Unknown.|T1098|AU-2;AU-12;SI-4|Generate the audit records needed to investigate the specific account-control path.|MS-HYBRID|Medium
002|A replication-rights exposure lacks required replication-abuse visibility|A confirmed unapproved replication-rights path exists AND required directory-service audit/SACL coverage or equivalent validated telemetry is absent.|KERBEROS.006 evidence; relevant audit configuration; domain SACL; sensor/forwarder health; approved monitoring requirement.|Restore the required replication monitoring and remove the unintended replication grant.|Absence of event 4662 during an idle window does not prove a gap. Alternative validated telemetry may satisfy the requirement.|T1003.006|AU-2;AU-6;AU-12|Support review of secret-replication activity along the identified permission path.||Medium
003|A controllable bridge lacks required PowerShell execution logging|A bridge control path exists AND its applicable PowerShell runtime lacks the required effective script-block/module logging or equivalent approved execution telemetry.|Underlying host path; PowerShell edition/version; effective logging policy; collection configuration; approved retention requirement.|Enable appropriate execution telemetry and protect potentially sensitive log content.|PowerShell 5.1 and 7 use different policy paths. This is a visibility gap, not evidence PowerShell was used maliciously.|T1059.001|AU-2;AU-12;SI-4|Preserve the execution evidence required for investigation on a sensitive host.|MS-PSLOG|Medium
004|A federation exposure lacks required issuer auditing|A confirmed federation-control path exists AND required issuer authentication or configuration-change telemetry is not generated or forwarded.|Issuer path; effective AD FS or supported issuer audit settings; forwarder state; independent destination; requirement.|Restore issuer auditing and forward it to a separately protected destination.|No denied sign-ins does not establish missing telemetry. Unsupported third-party issuer collection remains Unknown.|T1556.007;T1606.002|AU-2;AU-6;AU-12|Make changes to trusted authentication issuance reviewable.|MS-HYBRID|Medium
005|A synced cloud-policy administrator can act without required retained audit evidence|A source-controlled cloud identity can modify sensitive tenant policy AND required cloud audit retention or independent export coverage is absent.|Source path; effective policy permissions; tenant audit/export configuration; destination protection; licensing and retention requirement.|Protect cloud policy administration and retain the required audit evidence outside the same control path.|A missing premium license is not automatic noncompliance. Evaluate available retention and the organization's actual requirement.|T1484;T1685.002|AU-6;AU-9;AU-11|Retain and protect evidence of tenant policy changes across the identified boundary.||Medium
006|The same hybrid administrator can remove both host and cloud audit visibility|A verified principal/control chain can change host logging AND the cloud log destination or export configuration for the same hybrid path without independent approval.|Host logging-control rights; cloud export/storage rights; identity linkage; separation-of-duty and approval controls.|Separate log administration from the identities that control the monitored environment.|Host administrator status alone does not prove cloud audit deletion authority. Immutable retention and independent approval can break the chain.|T1685.001;T1685.002|AC-5;AU-9;SI-4|Protect audit information from the same administrative path it is intended to monitor.||High
007|A bridge protection gap coincides with a verified host-control route|A lower-trust control path reaches a bridge AND the organization's required endpoint or identity-sensor coverage is positively absent or unhealthy beyond its allowed window.|Underlying host path; authoritative coverage inventory; sensor heartbeat; service state; approved outage window.|Restore the required protection coverage and remove the unintended administrative route.|No license, no inventory permission, or no network response is not proof that a sensor is absent.|T1685|SI-3;SI-4;CA-7|Maintain required monitoring on assets exposed by the correlated path.||Medium
008|Clock and event-correlation gaps obscure a known cross-boundary path|A known hybrid path requires correlation of AD and cloud events AND verified time synchronization or retained timestamp metadata violates the defined investigation requirement.|Underlying path; effective time-source configuration; measured authorized time offset; event timestamp metadata; investigation tolerance.|Repair time synchronization and preserve UTC source timestamps across the relevant event sources.|Do not infer attacker timestomping. This mapping concerns a visibility limitation around valid-account activity, not observed adversary behavior.|T1078|AU-3;AU-8;AU-6|Keep cross-system audit records usable for reconstruction of the identified path.||Medium
